,title,link,text,language,family
0,Oto-Manguean languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oto-Manguean_languages,"The highest number of speakers of Oto-Manguean languages today is in the Mexican state of Oaxaca, where the two largest branches, the Zapotecan and the Mixtecan languages, are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined.","Zapotec, Mixtepec",Otomanguean
1,Mixtec languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixtec_languages,"Through music or the way in which certain rituals are carried out, popular Mixtec literature has survived as it did for millennia: by means of oral transmission. It was not until the 1990s that indigenous literature in Mexico took off again.","Zapotec, Mixtepec",Otomanguean
2,Oaxaca - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oaxaca,Contents · 1 Zapotecs; 3.3. · 2 Mixtecs; 3.3. · 3 Mazatecos; 3.3. · 4 Mixe; 3.3. · 5 Other.,"Zapotec, Mixtepec",Otomanguean
3,"Sacred Zapotec Chants | Monte Albán Ritual Music of the Stone ...Zapotec - Zapotec Song - YouTubeOto-Manguean languages - WikipediaZapotec Music - playlist by José Antonio Rodríguez Cortés ...Mixtec languages - WikipediaZapotec, San Agustín Mixtepec Language (ZTM) – L1 & L2 ...The Mixtecs and Zapotecs: Two Enduring Cultures of Oaxaca",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KuXJg-SxfPk,"Zapotec ritual music, ancient chants, and sacred drums from Monte Albán. Immerse yourself in ethereal voices, turtle shell percussion, bone flutes, and clay whistles echoing through the temples... Sep 26, 2021 · ll: I yeh I yeh I yeh I yeh, I yeh I yeh I yeh om I yeh I yeh I yeh I yeh, I yeh I yeh I yeh om. :ll ll: A per tul yu tu ra me ri mi a mo a mo the mo Oe met di a mi o ka ri mi shi mi lo o way tu a.... The highest number of speakers of Oto-Manguean languages today is in the Mexican state of Oaxaca, where the two largest branches, the Zapotecan and the Mixtecan languages, are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined. Zapotec Music · Playlist · 11 songs · 57 likes Through music or the way in which certain rituals are carried out, popular Mixtec literature has survived as it did for millennia: by means of oral transmission. It was not until the 1990s that indigenous literature in Mexico took off again. San Agustín Mixtepec Zapotec is an endangered indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Otomanguean language family and is part of the Zapotec macrolanguage. Sep 10, 2019 · It is no surprise that the Mixtecs and Zapotecs were neighbors as they both belong to the Oto-Manguean language family, which remains the largest linguistic group in the state of Oaxaca and in the Mexican Republic, represented by approximately 174 languages (according to Ethnologue.com). Who were the Mixtecs & Zapotecs? Joyce, Arthur A. Mixtecs, Zapotecs and Chatinos: Ancient Peoples of Southern Mexico. West Sussex, UK: John Wiley & Sons, 2010. How many Zapotec language variants are there? In recent publications, INALI has reported that there are 81 Mixtec language variants and 62 Zapotec language variants. As Zapotec and Mixtec communities became isolated from one another over the last couple thousand years, each language variant evolved in its own unique way. Who are the Zapotecs in Mexico? The Valley Zapotecs, who occupy the fertile Valley of Oaxaca in the center of the state. The Sierra Zapotecs (Mountain Zapotecs), who occupy the districts of Ixtlán, Villa Alta, and Choapán in the Sierra Norte. The Isthmus Zapotecs, who live on the tropical Isthmus of Tehuantepec, which is the narrowest part of the Mexican Republic. How many Zapotec people live in Oaxaca? If you count the various subsidiary Mixtec languages, the total Mixtec-speaking population of the Mexican Republic in 2000 included 444,498 individuals. The Zapotec ethnic group remains the largest indigenous group of Oaxaca and presently occupies 67 municipios of Oaxaca.","Zapotec, Mixtepec",Otomanguean
